{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 2. Related Work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.1 Prior decipherment attempts: all text, almost no pictures\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The modern history of Voynich scholarship begins with William Friedman and the First Voynich Manuscript Study Group of the 1940s (D\u8217'Imperio 1978); Kahn\u8217's {\i The Codebreakers} (Kahn 1967) provides the canonical historical frame for that generation\u8217's cryptanalytic methods. Friedman, who led the Signal Intelligence Service codebreaking effort against the Japanese PURPLE cipher, applied the same frequency-analytic tools to Voynichese without success. Captain Prescott Currier\u8217's transcription alphabet (Currier 1976) and Mary E. D\u8217'Imperio\u8217's monograph {\i The Voynich Manuscript: An Elegant Enigma} (D\u8217'Imperio 1978), produced under NSA auspices in 1978, together represent the first generation of sustained cryptanalytic engagement. They treated the manuscript as a cipher, assumed a source language, and sought a key. Neither succeeded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second wave is linguistic and statistical. Landini (Landini 2001) applied spectral analysis to Voynichese and reported word-length distributions consistent with natural language rather than random strings. Reddy and Knight (Reddy and Knight 2011) showed that Voynichese n-gram statistics fall within the range observed for known natural languages, undermining the stronger forms of the hoax hypothesis. Gordon Rugg (Rugg 2004) argued, by contrast, that a sixteenth-century hoaxer with a table-grille and a list of Voynichese syllables could produce statistically-natural-looking text, reinstating the hoax hypothesis as at least plausible. Stephen Bax (Bax 2014) claimed partial proper-noun readings for a handful of plant and star labels. Hauer and Kondrak (Hauer and Kondrak 2016) hypothesised that Voynichese is anagrammed Hebrew and reported plausible decodings of the manuscript\u8217's opening lines. Gerard Cheshire\u8217's 2019 proto-Romance claim (Cheshire 2019) was rapidly and unanimously rejected by the scholarly community (Fagin Davis 2019). The pattern is consistent: every generation of cryptanalytic and linguistic technology reaches for the manuscript, produces a partial and contested reading, and the field\u8217's consensus settles back into uncertainty.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Across all of this work, the illustrations appear mainly as anchor points for identifying which section is under discussion. They are visual {\i captions} for the text, never the object of analysis.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.2 Neural language models on Voynichese\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The arrival of large neural language models has added a new cohort of attempts. Montemurro and Zanette (Montemurro and Zanette 2013) applied information-theoretic keyword-clustering methods and demonstrated that Voynichese exhibits long-range word-frequency correlations characteristic of meaningful semantic content \u8212- the strongest quantitative evidence against the pure-hoax hypothesis. Character-level recurrent and transformer architectures have been explored in unpublished work, but none has produced a decipherment or a convincing generative model of the script, and the fundamental obstacle remains: all text-based approaches require a transcription layer \u8212- itself a source of systematic error, since there is no ground-truth character segmentation \u8212- and operate on a corpus of only approximately thirty-eight thousand tokens, well below the data requirements of modern neural language models. As in the earlier cryptanalytic waves, the text is the object and the illustrations are ignored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.3 Art-historical and non-computational visual analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A point worth being explicit about: the Voynich illustrations {\i have} been looked at carefully, for a long time, by serious readers. They have not been {\i computationally profiled}, and that is the gap we address; but it would misrepresent the field to suggest nobody has engaged with the imagery. Edith Sherwood has published extensive (and contentious) botanical identifications of the herbal illustrations (Sherwood 2008), attempting to match the Voynich plants to specimens from New World and Old World sources. Nicholas Pelling\u8217's {\i The Curse of the Voynich} (Pelling 2006) includes sustained visual analysis of the illustration programme, hand conventions, and iconographic anomalies. Ren\u233 ? Zandbergen\u8217's long-running reference site {\i voynich.nu} (Zandbergen 2004--2026) catalogues illustration types, pigment observations, and codicological detail at a level no published monograph matches. Lisa Fagin Davis\u8217's digital palaeography of the manuscript (Fagin Davis 2020) addresses quire structure and scribal hand distributions, both of which bear directly on how the sections were physically assembled. The Voynich Ninja online forum is the primary venue for ongoing scholarly discussion of the imagery. This work is qualitative and domain-expert; it is not computational in the sense of producing reproducible numerical profiles on every page. The contribution of the present paper is to introduce that complementary layer, not to supersede the art-historical tradition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.4 Computer vision in cultural heritage manuscripts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is, separately, a mature line of work applying computer vision to medieval and early-modern manuscripts. Document-image pipelines such as the DIVA-HisDB framework (Simistira et al. 2016) target layout analysis, character segmentation, and script classification. CNN-based manuscript dating (He et al. 2016; Studer et al. 2019) uses letterform morphology rather than page content. Style recognition in illuminated manuscripts and early books (Saleh and Elgammal 2016; Shen et al. 2019) has proven robust for attribution and period classification. IIIF-enabled analysis pipelines (Snydman et al. 2015) have made large facsimile collections computationally accessible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 None of this work, to our knowledge, has been applied to the Voynich Manuscript as a semantic analysis of its {\i imagery}. The existing computer-vision engagements with the Voynich Manuscript treat the illustrations as layout regions to be masked out so that Voynichese transcription is not polluted by pictorial ink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 More recently, the emergence of foundation vision-language models (Radford et al. 2021; Dosovitskiy et al. 2021) has enabled zero-shot and few-shot analysis of heritage imagery without task-specific fine-tuning. Computer-vision-for-art-history work from the \u201 ?cole des Ponts group \u8212- notably Shen, Pastrolin, Bounou, Gidaris, Smith, Poncet, and Aubry\u8217's large-scale historical watermark recognition (Shen et al. 2020), complementing their earlier pattern-retrieval work on art collections (Shen et al. 2019) \u8212- and computational-art-history work using human-pose foundation models, such as Bernasconi, Cetini\u263 ?, and Impett\u8217's hand-pose recognition dataset for early modern paintings (Bernasconi et al. 2023), together establish the methodological neighbourhood this paper works inside. Manovich\u8217's {\i Cultural Analytics} (Manovich 2020) is the broader methodological framing for scalable image-based cultural analysis. Our present work extends this line by applying a zero-shot VLM + archetype-lens methodology to a manuscript whose text channel has defeated prior analyses \u8212- a case where the visual channel is the last remaining signal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.5 The gap we address\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The contribution is narrow but, we believe, new. Prior art-historical work has {\i looked at} the illustrations at length; prior computational work has {\i looked past} them to the script. No prior work, to our knowledge, has submitted the Voynich illustrations to systematic computational visual-semantic profiling on a per-page basis with quantitative discrimination analysis against the scholarly section taxonomy. We ask that question, and we answer it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2.6 Distant viewing and the computational-humanities methodological lineage\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This paper sits inside a broader methodological conversation in the digital humanities about what computational methods can and cannot tell us about humanistic objects. The {\i distant reading} tradition (Moretti 2013; Jockers 2013; Piper 2018; Underwood 2019) asks what large-scale quantitative analysis of literary corpora reveals that close reading cannot; its image-side counterpart, {\i distant viewing} (Arnold and Tilton 2023), asks the same question of visual corpora. Our work is closer to distant viewing than to distant reading: the object of analysis is an image corpus, the method is computational, and the ambition is to surface structure that qualitative reading does not straightforwardly access. Johanna Drucker\u8217's {\i Graphesis} (Drucker 2014) is an essential companion to work of this shape \u8212- it asks what it commits us to, ontologically, to represent a humanistic object as a numerical vector; we take that question seriously in \u167 ?6.6. The present paper\u8217's specific contribution inside this lineage is narrow: we apply the distant-viewing methodology to a manuscript whose text has been the primary target of six centuries of attention and whose imagery has been systematically under-examined by computational methods.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 3. Method\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We describe the method at the level of principle. Implementation details \u8212- including the specific foundation model, the exact text of each dimension descriptor, and the production pipeline \u8212- are covered by a pending provisional patent and are available on request under appropriate research-use terms (\u167 ?7). The description below is sufficient to reproduce the spirit of the analysis; the specific numerical results reported in \u167 ?5 depend on the full implementation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.1 Visual semantic profiling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A visual semantic profile is a fixed-dimensional vector that measures, for each of a set of human-authored {\i archetype dimensions}, the semantic similarity between an image and a natural-language description of the dimension. The method has four components:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b A frozen vision-language foundation model.} The model maps both images and natural-language strings into a shared high-dimensional embedding space in which semantic similarity is approximately monotonic in cosine similarity. The model is used entirely zero-shot: no weights are updated, no fine-tuning is performed, and no exposure to Voynich imagery or medieval manuscript imagery enters the training pipeline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Sixteen archetype dimensions.} Each dimension is defined by a natural-language descriptor \u8212- a short paragraph in English written by the authors \u8212- that characterises, in ordinary prose, the visual content that the dimension is meant to capture. For the present analysis the sixteen dimensions are grouped into five thematic bands reflecting the expected content of an illustrated medieval codex: {\i natural world} (herbal/botanical, pharmaceutical/healing, natural/organic), {\i celestial} (celestial/astronomical, cosmological mapping, cyclical/seasonal, luminous/radiant), {\i esoteric} (alchemical transformation, ritualistic/ceremonial, encoded/hidden, supernatural/mystical), {\i biological} (anatomical/biological, aquatic/fluid, fertility/reproduction), and {\i structural} (geometric/mathematical, interconnected/networked). The precise wording of the descriptors is not disclosed in this paper. The thematic grouping and the human-readable labels are.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We flag a provenance subtlety here to be honest about what \u8220"zero-shot\u8221" means in this paper. The sixteen dimensions used for the primary analysis were authored {\i for} illustrated medieval codices, with the Voynich Manuscript as one of the motivating target documents. They were not tuned on the Voynich profile vectors, and no section labels entered the dimension-design process; but the thematic categories were chosen in knowledge of what illustrated medieval codices \u8212- including this one \u8212- are generally about. The honest framing is therefore: {\i supervised dimension design, zero-shot profile computation}. We do not claim the lens was authored in ignorance of its target. We claim that no Voynich image and no section label was used to tune the dimensions, and that the same dimensions applied unchanged to any illustrated medieval codex would still produce interpretable profiles. A lens-specificity control (\u167 ?5.6) addresses the stronger objection: we show that the same pipeline applied to two {\i unrelated} 16-d lenses \u8212- one designed for cross-cultural archaeological imagery, one designed for hermetic/alchemical symbolism \u8212- still recovers Voynich section structure, although more weakly than the medieval-codex lens does. This is the correct empirical answer to \u8220"did the lens decide the result?\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Dimension-design revision history (forking-paths disclosure).} In the spirit of pre-registration discipline, we disclose what we know about the design history of the sixteen dimensions. The reported sixteen dimensions are the {\i original} set authored for the medieval-codex lens; no dimension was added, dropped, renamed, or reweighted in response to observing per-section Voynich scores. The thematic-band grouping (natural world / celestial / esoteric / biological / structural) reflects the post-hoc convenience grouping presented in \u167 ?3.1 paragraph 2 and was assigned after the dimensions were frozen, but it carries no mathematical weight \u8212- every test in \u167 ?5 operates on the sixteen unweighted dimensions, not on the bands. We do not maintain a version-controlled revision history of intermediate dimension-set drafts that preceded the frozen sixteen, so we cannot enumerate iteration counts honestly; we therefore decline to claim \u8220"first authored set ever\u8221" and instead make the narrower verifiable claim: {\i the sixteen dimensions used for every number in this paper are the same sixteen dimensions in the same order, never modified after first profiling on the Voynich corpus}. A reader concerned about garden-of-forking-paths effects should read the lens-specificity control (\u167 ?5.4) \u8212- which uses two entirely independent dimension sets authored before the medieval-codex lens \u8212- and the random-prompt null protocol (\u167 ?5.12.1) as the empirical complement to this disclosure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Per-image profiling.} Each page image is encoded once by the vision model. For each of the sixteen dimensions, the descriptor is encoded by the text side of the same model. The profile entry for a given (image, dimension) pair is the cosine similarity between the two embeddings. The result, for a single page, is a 16-vector.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Section-level analysis.} Pages are grouped by the scholarly section label assigned in the Beinecke metadata. Statistical tests (\u167 ?5.2), classification (\u167 ?5.3), and visualisation (\u167 ?5.4, \u167 ?5.5) operate on the 16-d per-page vectors and the section labels. The section labels are used for {\i evaluation} of whether the profiles recover section structure. They are not used for {\i training} \u8212- the profiling system is strictly zero-shot and never sees a section label until after the profiles are computed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.2 What is {\i not} disclosed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To protect a pending provisional patent application, the following details are intentionally omitted:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The identity of the frozen foundation model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The exact text of the sixteen dimension descriptors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The training or distillation history of the embedding space.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The specific cosine-similarity normalisation and temperature scaling applied prior to profile comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab The complementary {\i dimension discovery} mode in which the system proposes latent dimensions from an unlabelled corpus (used in \u167 ?6 but not as the primary analysis).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Readers interested in replicating the results with their own vision-language models are encouraged to do so. The per-page profile vectors used for every number in this paper are released as a public dataset under CC BY-SA 4.0 (\u167 ?4).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We can, however, disclose the {\i class} of the foundation model without compromising the patent position: the model is a contemporary large-scale vision-language foundation model in the class of ViT-L/14 (or successor) architectures trained on web-scraped image\u8211-text pairs. Specifically, the model is a proprietary closed-weight model rather than an open-weight release; a reader interested in independently reproducing an equivalent analysis at the profile-generation layer could reasonably start from the OpenCLIP ViT-L/14 substrate used in the \u167 ?5.9 out-of-distribution probe as a comparable open-weight baseline. This training-corpus class weights Western, internet-era, English-language visual-linguistic conventions, and the limitations that class implies for recognising non-Western or pre-modern visual conventions apply to any zero-shot analysis built on it, including ours. We discuss the specific implications of this training-corpus class for Voynich-imagery analysis in \u167 ?6.6, and the political-economic dimension of foundation-model provenance in \u167 ?6.7.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 3.3 What the method does {\i not} do\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The method does not read Voynichese. It does not attempt to translate the manuscript into any human language. It does not label individual plant specimens with botanical names, individual stars with astronomical identifiers, or individual figures with identities. It does not produce a narrative interpretation of any page. It produces, for each page, a sixteen-number summary of where that page lands in a human-defined thematic space. That is all, and that is enough for the question we are asking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 4. Data\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4.1 Source\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All image data was obtained from the Yale University Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library\u8217's public IIIF manifest for the Voynich Manuscript, Beinecke MS 408 (Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library 2004). The manuscript is in the public domain (pre-1500) and the Beinecke facsimile is freely redistributable. The IIIF manifest lists 225 canvas entries covering the front and back covers, inside covers, flyleaves, fore-edge, spine, tail, head, and all 206 numbered or lettered pages. We downloaded the highest-resolution image available for each canvas (mean dimensions 2770 \u215 ? 3770 pixels) via the Beinecke IIIF endpoint in March 2026.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4.2 Corpus composition\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After download, we tagged each page image with a scholarly section label following the conventions established by Kennedy and Churchill (Kennedy and Churchill 2004) and maintained in the digital humanities literature (Clemens 2016; Zandbergen and Prinke 2016). The Voynich scholarly tradition, in Clemens, D\u8217'Imperio, Kennedy & Churchill, and Zandbergen\u8217's {\i voynich.nu}, typically divides the manuscript into {\i six} sections: herbal, astronomical/astrological, biological (ff.\u160 ?75r\u8211-84v, the nude-figures-in-pools pages), cosmological (ff.\u160 ?85r\u8211-86v, the unique nine-rosette foldout sometimes called the mappa mundi), pharmaceutical, and recipes / stars. We use this six-section division for the supplementary analysis reported in \u167 ?5.7. For the {\i primary} analysis in \u167 ?5.1\u8211-\u167 ?5.5 we adopt the coarser five-section partition distributed in the Beinecke IIIF metadata, in which the biological pages and the rosette foldout are merged under a single {\i cosmological / biological} label:\par}
