{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We exclude the nine unknown / cover / binding pages from all section-level analysis. They enter no ANOVA, no classifier, and no visualisation. They are retained only in the raw profile release because their profiles (encoded_hidden near the top; herbal_botanical near the bottom) offer a useful null-baseline contrast.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Defending the five-section merge.} A reader familiar with the manuscript may prefer the traditional six-section division in which the biological (bathing-figure) pages and the cosmological (rosette foldout) pages are kept separate. These sections are codicologically, iconographically, and thematically distinct: the biological pages are dominated by nude human figures in interconnected pools, and the four pages of the rosette foldout are arguably the most iconographically unique surface in the entire codex. D\u8217'Imperio (D\u8217'Imperio 1978) used the term {\i balneological} for the biological pages to emphasise their distinctness. We acknowledge the traditional division, and we report a six-section analysis in \u167 ?5.7 as a sanity check. We adopt the five-section merge for the primary analysis for three reasons. First, the rosette foldout contributes only four pages to the cosmological class \u8212- a sample too small for a reliable leave-one-out classification estimate in its own right. Second, the merge matches the Beinecke facsimile metadata, which is the canonical source and which a reader attempting to reproduce our analysis from the released profile files will encounter first. Third, the six-section analysis (\u167 ?5.7) confirms that all of our numerical results hold qualitatively: splitting biological from cosmological does not improve classification accuracy beyond what the merge achieves, because the rosette foldout pages cluster comfortably with the biological section in semantic space. Where this merge matters to scholarly interpretation, we flag it explicitly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4.3 Exclusions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The corpus metadata distributed with our public release lists every downloaded canvas. The nine covers / binding / flyleaf pages are flagged {\f1 section = unknown} and excluded from every statistical test. No illustrated page was excluded on the basis of content quality, page condition, or any other post-hoc filter. The \u8220"recipes / stars\u8221" section includes pages whose visual content is dominated by text \u8212- this is not a quality issue but a {\i feature} of that section, and as \u167 ?5.4 shows, the system correctly detects it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 4.4 Public release\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The per-page 16-d profile vectors, the section-level statistical tables, the cosine similarity matrix, and the UMAP coordinates computed in \u167 ?5 are released as {\f1 data/public/voynich_semantic_profiles/} under CC BY-SA 4.0 and archived permanently on Zenodo at DOI {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19560769"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
10.5281/zenodo.19560769
}}}
. No page images are redistributed: the Beinecke IIIF endpoint is the canonical source. Researchers who wish to reproduce the analysis with alternative vision-language models can do so against the published corpus metadata and the Beinecke canonical canvases.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 5. Results\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.1 Per-section archetype profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 2 shows the mean 16-d archetype profile for each of the five scholarly sections, drawn on a common radial scale, and a sixth panel with all five sections superimposed. The profiles are visually distinct. The herbal section peaks sharply on {\i herbal/botanical}, {\i natural/organic}, and {\i fertility/reproduction}. The astronomical section peaks on {\i celestial/astronomical}, {\i cyclical/seasonal}, {\i cosmological mapping}, and {\i luminous/radiant} \u8212- the four dimensions one would expect from pages dominated by concentric zodiac wheels and labelled stars. The cosmological/biological section peaks on {\i aquatic/fluid}, {\i anatomical/biological}, and {\i interconnected/networked} \u8212- again, exactly the dimensions a human reader would predict for the manuscript\u8217's bath scenes and plumbing diagrams. The pharmaceutical section looks like a slightly compressed herbal profile, as the confusion analysis in \u167 ?5.3 will confirm. The recipes / stars section peaks on {\i encoded/hidden} \u8212- because those pages are predominantly Voynichese text, and the system, working purely from pixels, correctly labels them as \u8220"a manuscript page densely covered in unknown or invented script.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We draw attention to the qualitative fit of the radar charts before reporting any statistical test. Most computational analyses of the Voynich Manuscript operate below the threshold of interpretability: the reader is told that some statistic passes some threshold, and is left to trust the pipeline. Here the raw mean profiles are legible to a human reader familiar with the manuscript. The system has recovered the manuscript\u8217's sections in a way that a Voynich scholar would describe as {\i right for the right reason}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2667\pich1832\picwgoal21336\pichgoal14656 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 2: Per-section mean archetype profiles and an all-sections overlay. Sixteen dimensions, drawn at common scale across all five sections.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.2 Dimension-level discrimination\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 3 summarises the dimension-by-section mean matrix in three complementary views: (a) raw mean cosine similarity; (b) row-normalised z-scores ({\i in which section does each dimension peak?}); and (c) column-normalised z-scores ({\i within each section, which dimensions peak?}). Both normalisation views show the diagonal structure of the effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw3223\pich1595\picwgoal25784\pichgoal12760 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 3: Dimension \u215 ? section profile matrix. (a) raw mean cosine similarity; (b) row-normalised z-score showing where each dimension peaks across sections; (c) column-normalised z-score showing the dominant dimensions within each section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 To test whether the section-level differences are reliably non-zero, we run three complementary tests on each of the sixteen dimensions:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b One-way ANOVA} ({\i F}-test), the parametric gold standard when within-section variance is roughly homoscedastic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Welch\u8217's robust one-way ANOVA}, which corrects the classical {\i F}-test for unequal within-group variances. The herbal section is more than nine times larger than the astronomical section, so the Welch correction is the textbook-appropriate test on this corpus.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Kruskal\u8211-Wallis} ({\i H}-test), a non-parametric test that makes no distributional assumption whatsoever.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alongside each significance test we report the classical effect size {\i \u951 ?}{\super 2}, which measures the fraction of total variance explained by section membership. Effect sizes are the honest way to report strength of association when {\i p}-values have all bottomed out on numerical noise, which on {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?197 they have.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b All sixteen dimensions discriminate between sections at {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15} under all three tests.} We report {\i p}-values capped at 10{\super \u8722 ?15}; smaller values are numerically meaningless because scipy\u8217's {\i F} and {\i H} distributions underflow well before that bound is reached, and the specific magnitudes below 10{\super \u8722 ?15} depend on machine epsilon rather than on the data. Table 1 gives the {\i F}-ratio, Welch {\i F}-ratio, Kruskal\u8211-Wallis {\i H}, and {\i \u951 ?}{\super 2} for all sixteen dimensions, ranked by classical {\i F}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Table 1.} All sixteen archetype dimensions ranked by one-way ANOVA {\i F}-ratio across the five scholarly sections. Classical and Welch {\i F}-ratios agree qualitatively in every row. Effect size {\i \u951 ?}{\super 2} reports the fraction of total variance explained by section membership. All sixteen dimensions are significant at {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15} under all three tests. {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?197 pages; degrees of freedom = (4, 192) for classical {\i F}.\par}
