{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three findings. First, the voynich lens is the strongest of the three \u8212- as one would expect from a lens authored for illustrated medieval codices. This is mostly a calibration result: the dimensions were chosen with content like this in mind. Second, and more importantly, {\i the archaeology and cryptological lenses also recover section structure at rates well above chance}. The cross-cultural archetype dimensions and the hermetic esoteric dimensions both produce classifiers that clear the majority-class baseline. The section signal is not a property of the lens we chose; it is a property of the manuscript itself, and any reasonably-constructed 16-d lens picks up a measurable amount of it. Third, the voynich lens\u8217's margin over the other two is substantial but not enormous. The right way to read this is: {\i the manuscript has enough thematic structure to be visible through multiple viewpoints, and authoring a target-appropriate lens sharpens but does not create the signal.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.5 Semantic-space topology (UMAP and PCA)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 4 shows three low-dimensional projections of the 16-d archetype profile matrix. Panel (a) is a UMAP scatter in axes 1 \u215 ? 2; panel (b) is the same UMAP in axes 2 \u215 ? 3, which gives a clearer view of the pharmaceutical-inside-herbal geometry than the top-down view; and panel (c) is a deterministic linear PCA projection that acts as a sanity check against the possibility that UMAP is inventing the clustering. In all three projections the five sections separate spontaneously without any supervision \u8212- the dimensionality-reduction step sees only the 16-d profile vectors and is never told the section labels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2860\pich1065\picwgoal22880\pichgoal8520 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 4: Low-dimensional projection of 16-d archetype profiles. (a) UMAP axes 1 \u215 ? 2; (b) UMAP axes 2 \u215 ? 3; (c) linear PCA as deterministic sanity check. Colour encodes scholarly section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We also report {\b UMAP seed stability}. Non-linear dimensionality reduction is famously seed-dependent, so we repeat the UMAP projection across 10 random seeds and measure the Procrustes-aligned residual RMSE of each seed\u8217's projection against the reference projection. Figure 9 shows the first four seeds side by side, and we report the mean normalised RMSE across the full ensemble. The clusters persist and the global topology is stable; projection-specific details (the exact orientation of the herbal manifold in the UMAP frame) are not stable across seeds, as expected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2862\pich749\picwgoal22896\pichgoal5992 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 9: UMAP robustness across four random seeds (of ten tested). Cluster assignment is stable; exact cluster orientation is seed-dependent, as is normal for UMAP.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two observations on the projected structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b The herbal cluster is internally structured.} The 118 herbal pages do not form a single blob but a visible elongated distribution. The Voynich Manuscript\u8217's herbal section is known to contain at least two sub-styles of botanical illustration, sometimes called Herbal A and Herbal B, which were first distinguished by Currier (Currier 1976) as {\i scribal hand} categories rather than illustration styles \u8212- the distinction is palaeographic. Later scholars have noted that the hand distinction correlates with some illustration characteristics, and {\i voynich.nu} (Zandbergen 2004--2026) maintains a running list of observed correspondences. We do not attempt to resolve the hand/style correlation in this paper; we only report that the system has picked up {\i some} kind of internal structure in the herbal section, and that this structure is a natural candidate for comparison against Currier\u8217's published folio assignments in future work. We note the standard UMAP caveat: local density in a UMAP embedding depends on the {\f1 min_dist} and {\f1 n_neighbors} hyperparameters \u8212- different settings produce visibly different cluster geometries even on the same input \u8212- so the elongated-distribution observation in the herbal cluster should be read as illustrative of {\i some} internal variation rather than as a guarantee of {\i which} variation. The PCA panel (Figure 4c, deterministic) is the load-bearing geometric evidence per \u167 ?6.6; the UMAP elongation is consistent with sub-structure that further analysis can resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b The pharmaceutical cluster sits inside the herbal cluster.} The pharmaceutical section is not a separate blob but a lobe adjacent to the herbal manifold \u8212- which is the visual dual of the classifier confusion we reported in \u167 ?5.3. The UMAP and the logistic regression are showing the same underlying geometry.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.6 Cross-section semantic distance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 5 gives the cross-section distance structure in 16-d archetype space, rendered two ways: (a) as the cosine {\i distance} (1\u8197 ?\u8722 ?\u8197 ?cos\u8198 ?) between section centroids, which lets the full dynamic range of the between-section structure render visible; and (b) as the neighbour-rank view, which asks {\i for each section, which other section is its nearest, second-nearest, \u8230 ?, neighbour in archetype space?}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2322\pich1004\picwgoal18576\pichgoal8032 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 5: Cross-section distance structure. (a) cosine distance between section centroids (full dynamic range); (b) nearest-neighbour rank per section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The five sections share most of their archetype signal: cosine similarities between section centroids are all above 0.93, which means the cosine-{\i distance} range is narrow. This is not a problem but an important observation. The Voynich Manuscript is a single physical object drawn in a single style by a small number of hands on a single pool of vellum. The system should detect this shared substrate, and it does. The between-section variation we test in \u167 ?5.2 is {\i within} the narrow band of manuscript-wide similarity, and yet it is strong enough to discriminate every section at {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15} and to drive the classifier accuracy reported in \u167 ?5.3. The sections are distinguishable {\i in spite of} a dominant shared manuscript style, not because they live in disjoint semantic regions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reading the distance matrix: {\b herbal and pharmaceutical are the most similar pair}, matching the classifier and UMAP results. {\b Astronomical and pharmaceutical are the least similar pair} \u8212- intuitively, zodiac wheels look nothing like apothecary vessels. {\b Cosmological/biological is the most \u8220"central\u8221" section}, with the highest mean similarity to the other four, which matches its scholarly positioning as the heart of the manuscript\u8217's thematic activity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.7 Supplementary: six-section analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 As a sanity check on the five-section merge defended in \u167 ?4.2, we repeat the classifier analysis using the traditional six-section partition in which the bathing-figure pages (ff.\u160 ?75r\u8211-84v) are labelled {\i biological} (n = 19) and the rosette foldout (ff.\u160 ?85r\u8211-86v) is labelled {\i cosmological} (n = 4). The split is resolved directly from folio numbers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Voynich 16-d + Pipeline LR on the six-section split, LOOCV: 89.85%} (Wilson 95% CI [84.8%, 93.3%]).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The six-section classifier is qualitatively equivalent to the five-section classifier. The separation into biological vs cosmological does not harm overall accuracy and \u8212- as \u167 ?4.2 predicted \u8212- the four rosette-foldout pages cluster with the biological section in archetype space. The five-section merge is therefore a convenient rather than a distorting choice, and the results reported in \u167 ?5.1\u8211-\u167 ?5.6 are robust to this partition.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.8 Qualitative error analysis\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 8 shows the pharmaceutical pages misclassified as herbal by the LOOCV classifier. Each column contains the page thumbnail and its 16-d profile overlaid on both the herbal and pharmaceutical centroids. The pattern is consistent: these are pages on which the botanical specimen occupies most of the visual surface, while the apothecary vessel is small, marginal, or absent. The system is detecting the plant, as a classifier operating on pixels should. This is the most useful figure in the paper for understanding what the method measures and where it breaks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw3212\pich1330\picwgoal25696\pichgoal10640 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 8: Qualitative error analysis \u8212- the pharmaceutical pages misclassified as herbal. Each column: page thumbnail (top) and 16-d profile (bottom) overlaid on the herbal centroid (green dashed) and the pharmaceutical centroid (orange dashed).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.9 Out-of-distribution sanity check\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other methodologically necessary question: {\i does the voynich lens fire sensibly on manuscripts that are not the Voynich?} If it does not \u8212- if the lens is essentially a \u8220"this is the Voynich\u8221" detector rather than a semantic lens \u8212- then the discrimination result reported in \u167 ?5.3 is a property of the lens, not of the manuscript. To rule this out we apply the voynich lens to three comparison corpora and one control sample of Voynich pages, all processed through the {\b same local image-text pipeline}: an off-the-shelf OpenCLIP ViT-L/14 encoder ({\f1 openai} pretrained weights), cosine similarity against the sixteen public {\f1 voynich.yaml} dimension descriptors, no post-processing. This local pipeline is {\i not} the production xenoglyph profiling system \u8212- the absolute score magnitudes are therefore not directly comparable to the values in {\f1 voynich_profiles.json} \u8212- but because all four corpora in this section are processed through the {\i same} local pipeline, the cross-corpus comparison is internally consistent and the qualitative pattern is what we are after.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three comparison corpora are:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Tacuinum Sanitatis} (Codex Vindobonensis series nova 2644, \u214 ?sterreichische Nationalbibliothek, Vienna, c.\u160 ?1390\u8211-1400). The same-era, same-domain peer to the Voynich \u8212- a medieval illustrated herbal/medical manuscript with plant, food, and remedy miniatures. If the voynich lens is a legitimate medieval-codex lens rather than a Voynich-specific detector, it should fire on {\i herbal/botanical}, {\i pharmaceutical/healing}, and {\i natural/organic} when shown a Tacuinum plant page. Small sample: we were rate-limited by the Wikimedia Commons API during download, and the analysis in this section uses {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?3 Tacuinum pages. We acknowledge the sample size; the result is directional evidence, not a statistical claim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Codex Seraphinianus} (Luigi Serafini, 1981). A modern, intentionally opaque illustrated codex in an invented script with surreal botanical-and-zoological imagery. {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?30 pages sampled from the 50-page corpus in {\f1 data/raw/comparison_manuscripts/seraphinianus/}. The correct behaviour here is partial overlap with the Voynich on {\i encoded/hidden} (both are in invented scripts) and distinct signatures on the content dimensions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Rohonc Codex} (anonymous, 18th-century Hungary). An undeciphered illustrated manuscript with religious-iconographic content. {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?30 pages from {\f1 data/raw/comparison_manuscripts/rohonc/}. Again: we expect a high {\i encoded/hidden} score and a distinct content signature.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 And the control sample:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab {\b Voynich (local pipeline)}. {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?30 pages randomly sampled from the 197-page analysed corpus, re-profiled through the same local CLIP pipeline used for the three OOD corpora. This is the critical apples-to-apples reference \u8212- it tells us what a \u8220"Voynich profile\u8221" {\i looks like in this pipeline}, which is what we need to compare against.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Figure 11 shows the result.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw2858\pich1244\picwgoal22864\pichgoal9952 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Figure 11: Out-of-distribution sanity check. Mean voynich-lens profile per corpus under the same local CLIP pipeline. Left: radar overlay. Right: row-normalised z-scores (which dimensions peak within each corpus). Tacuinum Sanitatis (medieval herbal peer) peaks on the herbal/pharmaceutical/fertility band as expected. Voynich peaks on the same band but more sharply. Seraphinianus and Rohonc both peak on encoded/hidden, correctly identifying their undeciphered-script content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three observations.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b The voynich lens fires correctly on a non-Voynich herbal manuscript.} The Tacuinum Sanitatis profile peaks on {\i pharmaceutical/healing}, {\i fertility/reproduction}, and {\i herbal/botanical} \u8212- the same three-dimension cluster that dominates the Voynich herbal and pharmaceutical sections. This is the existence-proof result Reviewer B asked for: the lens is not a \u8220"this is the Voynich\u8221" detector; it is a medieval-codex content lens that fires on botanical content regardless of which manuscript produced it. We emphasise that {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?3 is too small for a statistical claim, but the pattern is directionally what the lens is supposed to produce, and a larger Tacuinum replication is a trivial follow-up we intend to run after submission.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Voynich\u8217's herbal/botanical peak is sharper than Tacuinum\u8217's.} Under the same pipeline, the Voynich control sample scores higher on {\i herbal/botanical} than the Tacuinum sample does. We read this as an artefact of visual composition: the Voynich herbal pages place a single plant on otherwise-blank parchment, while the Tacuinum miniatures embed plants in three-quarter-page harvest/preparation scenes with multiple figures and architectural backgrounds. The Tacuinum\u8217's herbal signal is real but dilute because the composition is narrative; the Voynich\u8217's is concentrated because the composition is specimen-on-background. This is a content observation, not a lens failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab {\b Seraphinianus and Rohonc both peak on {\i encoded/hidden}, not on {\i herbal/botanical}.} The row-normalised z-score panel of Figure 11 makes the distinction crisp: both undeciphered-script codices land their relative peak on the dimension that was written to describe \u8220"manuscript pages densely covered in unknown or invented script.\u8221" The Voynich encoded/hidden score is high in absolute terms (Voynich has dense Voynichese text like the others) but it is {\i not} the relative peak because the Voynich herbal/botanical signal is higher still. In Seraphinianus and Rohonc there is no comparably strong botanical signal to compete with, so the encoded/hidden dimension wins by default. This is exactly the behaviour an honest lens should exhibit.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Taken together with the lens-specificity control in \u167 ?5.4, the OOD probe addresses the two forms of the \u8220"is the result an artefact of the lens?\u8221" objection from different directions. The lens-specificity control shows that three independent lenses all recover section structure on the Voynich. The OOD probe shows that the voynich lens produces appropriate content-level profiles on non-Voynich manuscripts. Together they defend the claim that {\i both} the lens and the data carry real signal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.10 Head-to-head: text channel vs visual channel\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most important question a methodologically skeptical reader could ask of this paper is the one we have so far {\i avoided}: if the illustrations recover the sections at 90%, what does the {\i text} do? The field has been trying to read the text for six hundred years without success, but the text-recovery question is not \u8220"can you decipher Voynichese\u8221" \u8212- it is \u8220"can a classifier that does not attempt to read Voynichese still recover the scholarly section labels from the character-level statistics of the script?\u8221" This is a narrower and answerable question, and it is the one a CV reviewer has the right to ask.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We therefore report a per-page Voynichese character-n-gram baseline. We use Takeshi Takahashi\u8217's complete EVA transcription of Beinecke MS 408, as distributed in the LSI IVTFF archive on {\i voynich.nu} (Zandbergen 2004--2026). For each of the 182 analysed pages that have both a visual profile and a complete Takahashi transcription, we extract per-page character n-grams ({\i n}\u8196 ?\u8712 ?\u8196 ?\{1,\u8198 ?2,\u8198 ?3\}) under a TF-IDF weighting. We fit the same Pipeline-wrapped multinomial logistic regression under the same leave-one-out protocol used for the visual classifier \u8212- and for strict apples-to-apples, we also rerun the visual 16-d archetype classifier, the visual raw 768-d embedding classifier, and the handcrafted 6-d layout-feature classifier on this same 182-page subset. All four feature spaces therefore share an identical training protocol and an identical sample.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The text-channel pipeline is {\f1 Pipeline(TfidfVectorizer, LogisticRegression)}: the TF-IDF vectorizer is fit {\i inside} the sklearn Pipeline on the training fold of each leave-one-out split, not on the full 182-page corpus before cross-validation. No term-frequency or inverse-document-frequency statistic is computed across held-out pages before CV. This is the analogous no-leakage discipline to the scaler-leakage protocol disclosed in Appendix B.1 \u8212- it is the canonical text-pipeline failure mode and is the reason the Pipeline wrapper is non-negotiable.\par}
