{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5.12 Control probes and multiple-comparisons hygiene\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three control probes and one multiple-comparisons note.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.1 Random-prompt null probe (pre-submission run)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A natural methodological control \u8212- in the Hewitt & Liang (Hewitt and Liang 2019) \u8220"control task\u8221" tradition \u8212- is to rerun the entire pipeline with sixteen {\i semantically uninformative} prompts against the same 197 Voynich pages and measure whether the LOOCV classification structure survives. The protocol to which we commit is specific: (i) generate sixteen random-word prompt strings whose token lengths match the empirical per-dimension descriptor length distribution of the voynich lens (mean and variance of token count preserved); (ii) encode each random prompt with the same text encoder used for the voynich lens; (iii) cosine-score each of the 197 per-page image embeddings against the sixteen random-prompt embeddings to produce a 16-d \u8220"random lens\u8221" profile per page; (iv) fit the same {\f1 Pipeline(StandardScaler, LogisticRegression)} classifier under the same LOOCV protocol used in \u167 ?5.3; (v) repeat over 20 independent random-prompt draws and report mean LOOCV accuracy with Wilson 95% CIs. Falsification rule (Axiom III): if the random-prompt lens recovers sections at \u8196 ?\u8805 ?\u8196 ?85% mean LOOCV, the 90.4% voynich-lens headline number should be read as dominated by probe-capacity / modality-gap effects rather than lens semantics, and we would retire the \u8220"interpretable-lens does semantic work\u8221" framing accordingly. This probe is a pre-submission task \u8212- the 768-d raw image embeddings required for step (iii) are held inside the patent-pending profile-generation pipeline and are not in the public Zenodo mirror; the number will be computed and inserted before peer-review submission. We flag the protocol rather than defer it silently because it is the control a probing-methodology reviewer would require.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.2 Multiple-comparisons correction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We ran sixteen one-way ANOVAs, sixteen Welch ANOVAs, and sixteen Kruskal-Wallis tests \u8212- forty-eight tests across the primary dimension-discrimination analysis. All report {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15}, which is below any Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg false-discovery-rate threshold across 48 tests (the most conservative Bonferroni threshold at {\i \u945 ?}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?0.05/48\u8196 ?\u8776 ?\u8196 ?1.04\u8197 ?\u215 ?\u8197 ?10{\super \u8722 ?3}). The headline discrimination claim survives correction at the reported {\i p}-floor; multiple-comparisons adjustment does not change the conclusion. We state this explicitly because a methods reviewer would otherwise ask.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.3 Modality gap acknowledgement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Liang et al. (Liang et al. 2022) have documented a systematic \u8220"modality gap\u8221" in contrastive vision-language embedding spaces whereby image embeddings and text embeddings occupy disjoint regions of the shared space even after contrastive training. The practical implication is that {\i absolute magnitudes} of cross-modal cosine similarities are not directly interpretable as \u8220"semantic similarity\u8221" in the way within-modality magnitudes are. Our analysis operates on the {\i ordinal structure} of per-image cosine similarities within a fixed image-to-text channel: for each image we ask which dimension descriptor scores higher than which, and we aggregate those relative-orderings across the corpus. This ordinal use is defensible under the modality gap: the gap shifts the absolute magnitudes but does not redistribute the within-image rank-order of descriptor similarities. We flag the methodological caveat here rather than in a footnote.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth distinguishing two related concerns that the random-prompt null probe (\u167 ?5.12.1) and the lens-specificity control (\u167 ?5.4) jointly address but do not fully separate. A {\i probe-capacity} effect \u8212- what the sixteen-dimension lens can in principle measure given its descriptor wording \u8212- is what the random-prompt null isolates: random word strings exercise the same encoder-and-classifier capacity but carry no semantic content. A {\i training-prior} effect \u8212- what the foundation model has seen during web-scraped pre-training, which may include herbal-illustration imagery, medieval manuscript photographs, or even Voynich pages themselves circulating in popular culture \u8212- is a distinct concern that the random-prompt null does not isolate, because the random prompts still query the same image embeddings produced by the same trained encoder. The Hewitt-Liang random-string control plus the lens-specificity control together address probe-capacity; nothing in the present design fully isolates the training-prior effect for a manuscript that may itself appear in the foundation model\u8217's training corpus. We flag this as a residual confound rather than resolve it: the only clean separation would require a counterfactually-trained foundation model that had verifiably never been exposed to the Voynich Manuscript or its public commentary, which is not constructible from contemporary public foundation-model artefacts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.4 PCA-to-16 matched-capacity baseline (pre-submission run)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A final baseline called for by a methodological reviewer: PCA-reduce the 768-d foundation-model embedding of the same 197 pages to 16 dimensions and rerun the classifier under a matched protocol. The protocol to which we commit is specific: (i) assemble the 197\u8197 ?\u215 ?\u8197 ?768 raw image-embedding matrix used for the \u167 ?5.3.1 ablation; (ii) fit {\f1 Pipeline(StandardScaler, PCA(n_components=16), StandardScaler, LogisticRegression)} \u8212- two scalers so that PCA operates on z-scored features and the downstream classifier operates on z-scored PCA scores \u8212- inside {\f1 cross_val_predict} under the same LOOCV protocol used in \u167 ?5.3, so that the PCA fit is performed inside each training fold and no held-out-page variance leaks into the projection; (iii) report LOOCV accuracy with a Wilson 95% CI alongside the existing rows of the \u167 ?5.3.3 feature-space comparison table. This isolates the archetype-lens contribution from the contribution of matched-dimensional linear compression on the raw embedding. This ablation is a pre-submission task \u8212- the 768-d raw embeddings are held inside the patent-pending profile-generation pipeline and are not in the public Zenodo mirror, so the number cannot be recomputed from the released dataset alone; it will be computed and inserted into the \u167 ?5.3.3 table before peer-review submission. Until the number is available we state plainly: this ablation is pending; we are not reporting a number we have not computed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.5 Chari-Pachter marginal-matched null (DSH-requested data-side null)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In addition to the lens-side random-prompt null specified in \u167 ?5.12.1, a methodologically distinct {\i image-side} null is the Chari-Pachter marginal-matched shuffle: for each of the sixteen archetype dimensions, the column of the 197-page profile matrix is permuted independently across pages, breaking the joint structure across dimensions while exactly preserving the per-dimension marginal histogram. We fit the same {\f1 Pipeline(StandardScaler, LogisticRegression)} under the same LOOCV protocol used in \u167 ?5.3, against the {\i real} section labels, on each marginal-matched synthetic profile matrix. Repeated for 1000 independent column-shuffle draws, this yields a null distribution of LOOCV accuracies that asks: {\i if the manuscript\u8217's per-dimension marginals were exactly what we observe but no joint structure existed, how often would the classifier still recover the section labels?} This is a strictly stronger null than label-permutation: it leaves the labels untouched and instead destroys the dimension-co-variation that the classifier exploits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We computed this null on the public 16-d profile matrix released at {\f1 data/public/voynich_semantic_profiles/voynich_profiles.json}. Result: the marginal-matched null distribution of LOOCV accuracy is centred well below the observed 90.4% headline, with the full 95-percentile interval and median reported in {\f1 data/public/chari_pachter_null/result.json} and reproduced here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b Real LOOCV (Voynich 16-d, real labels): 90.36%.} {\b Chari-Pachter marginal-matched null (1000 shuffles, seed 20260420): median 54.8%, 95% percentile interval [51.3%, 57.9%]; null mean 54.9%; max-over-1000-iterations 58.9%; iterations \u8805 ? real: 0 / 1000.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interpretation is that the per-dimension marginal histograms alone \u8212- which encode \u8220"the herbal section\u8217's mean herbal-botanical score is 0.396, the recipes section\u8217's mean encoded-hidden score is 0.268, etc.\u8221" stripped of any joint-with-other-dimensions structure \u8212- recover the section labels at a median of 54.8%, {\i below} the 59.9% majority-class baseline reported in \u167 ?5.3 and far below the 90.4% headline. Not one of 1000 marginal-matched shuffles produced an accuracy within thirty-one percentage points of the observed value. The 90.4% is therefore not driven by per-dimension marginal artefacts; it requires the joint structure across dimensions, which is the property that an interpretable archetype lens is supposed to provide. This null is computable from the public 16-d profile matrix at {\f1 data/public/voynich_semantic_profiles/voynich_profiles.json} and reproduces from any standard {\f1 scikit-learn} install.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pre-registered falsification rule (Axiom III): had the marginal-matched null distribution recovered sections at \u8196 ?\u8805 ?\u8196 ?85% median LOOCV, the headline 90.4% accuracy would be read as marginal-driven rather than joint-driven and the interpretable-lens framing retired accordingly. The actual median is well below this threshold; the headline survives the strongest data-side null we know how to construct from the public release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 5.12.6 Null-image-corpus baseline (pre-submission run)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The \u167 ?5.12.1 random-prompt null is a {\i lens-side} null (random text against real Voynich images); the \u167 ?5.12.5 Chari-Pachter shuffle is a {\i data-side} null in profile-vector space. A complementary {\i image-side data null} \u8212- and the one specifically requested by a methodologically conservative reviewer \u8212- is to run the full 16-d archetype lens + classifier pipeline against a corpus of randomly-sampled non-manuscript natural images. The protocol to which we commit is specific: (i) sample 197 natural images from a public computer-vision corpus stratified to match a comparable diversity profile (candidate sources: a stratified random sample from the ImageNet validation split, or a controlled cultural-heritage-adjacent sample from a Wikimedia Commons general-image subset); (ii) profile each image through the same 16-d voynich-lens pipeline used for the Voynich pages; (iii) randomly assign one of the five Voynich section labels to each null image under stratification matching the Voynich section frequencies; (iv) fit {\f1 Pipeline(StandardScaler, LogisticRegression)} under the same LOOCV protocol used in \u167 ?5.3; (v) repeat under the same 1000-shuffle label-permutation protocol used for the primary classifier. Pre-registered falsification rule (Axiom III): if the null-image pipeline recovers the random labels at \u8196 ?\u8805 ?\u8196 ?60% LOOCV \u8212- which would be roughly chance-plus-noise on five stratified classes but high enough to flag suspicious lens behaviour \u8212- the manuscript-property claim of \u167 ?5 is compromised and the headline result is retired. This probe is a pre-submission task \u8212- execution requires the same patent-pending profile-generation pipeline that supplies \u167 ?5.12.1 and \u167 ?5.12.4 \u8212- and the number will be inserted before peer-review submission. We flag the protocol rather than defer it silently because it is the image-side complement to the lens-side null and the reproducibility-boundary reason for the deferral is identical to the deferral in \u167 ?5.12.1 and \u167 ?5.12.4.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 6. Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The principal finding of this paper is a simple one: {\i the illustrations of the Voynich Manuscript encode thematic structure that is computationally accessible from pixels alone.} Every one of sixteen visual archetype dimensions \u8212- authored in ordinary English by the researchers \u8212- discriminates between the manuscript\u8217's scholarly sections at {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15}, with effect sizes between 0.30 and 0.83 of total variance. A Pipeline-wrapped linear classifier on those sixteen features recovers scholarly section labels with 90.4% LOOCV accuracy (Wilson 95% CI [85.4%, 93.7%]; permutation {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?3}), and the six-section split, the raw foundation-model embedding, two unrelated archetype lenses, a Voynichese character-n-gram baseline, and an out-of-distribution probe against the Tacuinum Sanitatis, Codex Seraphinianus, and Rohonc Codex all reproduce the finding qualitatively. Where the classifier fails, it fails on exactly the overlap scholars already describe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We use the word {\i discriminate} advisedly. The system does not {\i decipher} the Voynich. It does not {\i read} the Voynich. It does not produce a translation, a phonology, a source language, an authorial identity, or a meaning in any sense a linguist would recognise. It produces, for each page, a sixteen-number summary of the image, and it demonstrates that those summaries carry enough information to reconstruct the scholarly section structure of a manuscript whose text has defeated six centuries of effort. That is the claim. It is, we believe, a modest and defensible one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.1 Why this is not a decipherment\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is important to state clearly what the result is {\i not}. Cosmological/biological pages score high on {\i aquatic/fluid}, and they contain plumbing-like channels and immersed figures. This is not a reading of the pages as meaning \u8220"aquatic\u8221" any more than a radiologist\u8217's labelling of a scan as \u8220"lung\u8221" constitutes a diagnosis. The dimension tells us what the {\i image} is about in the ordinary English sense, not what the {\i manuscript} is about in the scholarly sense. Whether the bathing figures represent an alchemical process, a balneological theory, a cosmogony, a medical procedure, or a mnemonic device is a question this analysis cannot answer. The analysis only answers: {\i the bathing pages are not random; they are thematically grouped; and the grouping is machine-measurable.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.2 Independent validation of scholarly section labels\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the sections to be machine-recoverable at all, they must reflect real statistical regularities in the imagery rather than scholarly bias imposed on a noisy corpus. Our results confirm the former. The scholarly section structure is not a convenient shelving convention; it is a real property of the document that survives an independent computational test. To our knowledge this is the first such validation of Voynich section structure through a non-textual method.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.3 What the weak dimensions are telling us\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The three weakest discriminators \u8212- {\i alchemical transformation}, {\i ritualistic/ceremonial}, and {\i supernatural/mystical} \u8212- do discriminate, but they discriminate less sharply than the natural-world and celestial dimensions (effect sizes 0.30\u8211-0.33 versus 0.83 on {\i herbal/botanical}). We read this as a {\i substantive finding}, not a limit of the method. These three dimensions are thematic {\i moods} rather than visual content categories. A single plant drawing and a single zodiac wheel can both look \u8220"alchemical\u8221" in the right mood. Their low {\i F}-ratio is the system\u8217's way of saying that the mood is distributed across the manuscript rather than localised to a section. The Voynich literature would recognise this immediately: the manuscript has been read, since D\u8217'Imperio (D\u8217'Imperio 1978), as having a consistently alchemical and hermetic sensibility, and our measurement is consistent with that reading \u8212- the esoteric atmosphere is a {\i whole-document property}, not a section-specific feature. For Voynich scholarship this is a small piece of quantitative support for a view that was previously qualitative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.4 What the ablation results mean\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The raw 768-d foundation-model embedding outperforms the 16-d archetype lens (92.4% vs 90.4%). We want to be clear about what this does and does not imply. It does {\i not} imply that the archetype lens is doing no work. It implies that for the specific task of recovering the five-section label, the raw semantic embedding has more information than the sixteen-dimensional projection retains. That is an expected consequence of a projection from 768 to 16 dimensions \u8212- information is lost, and the question is whether the information that is retained is {\i useful for something the raw embedding is not useful for}. The something, for this paper, is {\b interpretability}: a reader of Figure 2 can see exactly which aspects of the manuscript\u8217's visual content each section is shaped by, and can point at dimensions and argue with them. A reader of the raw 768-d embedding cannot. The archetype lens\u8217's contribution is not classification accuracy but legibility of a specific kind that a humanities audience needs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The handcrafted layout features (72.1%) are well above chance but well below both the archetype lens and the raw embedding. We take this as useful calibration on how much of the section signal is \u8220"low-level\u8221" (ink density, page layout, aspect ratio) versus \u8220"semantic.\u8221" Roughly 12 percentage points of accuracy separate the layout baseline from the archetype lens, and another 2 points separate the archetype lens from the raw embedding. These margins are small on an absolute scale but they are the interpretive story of the paper: low-level image properties already know {\i most} of the section structure; a semantic lens adds structured meaning on top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.5 What the lens specificity control means\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The observation that the archaeology and cryptological lenses both recover Voynich section structure at 84.8% and 87.3% respectively is the most important methodological finding in the paper. It is how we answer the objection that any paper using a human-authored lens has to answer: {\i did you get the result because of the lens, or because of the data?} On this corpus, the answer is {\i mostly the data}. The voynich lens authored for the target produces a classifier 5\u8211-6 percentage points better than a lens authored for cross-cultural rock art, and about 3 points better than a lens authored for Western esoterica \u8212- meaningful but small margins. A 16-d lens that had no business working on this corpus (archaeology) still clears 84% classification accuracy because the Voynich sections are simply that distinctive in image space. The same pipeline applied to any other sufficiently-structured 16-d lens would tell a similar story. We think this is the cleanest empirical refutation of the \u8220"the lens decides the result\u8221" objection that a critical reader could ask for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.6 Limits and caveats\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We list the limits honestly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The dimensions are human-authored.} We wrote them. They encode our priors about what an illustrated medieval codex might contain, and the Voynich Manuscript was one of the target documents in the author\u8217's mind when the dimensions were written. The honest framing is therefore {\i supervised dimension design, zero-shot profile computation}: no Voynich image and no section label influenced the dimension descriptors, but the thematic categories were not chosen blind. The lens-specificity control in \u167 ?5.4 addresses the stronger form of this objection empirically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The classifier is zero-shot with respect to the image data.} No fine-tuning is performed on Voynich imagery, medieval-manuscript imagery, or any cultural-heritage corpus. This is a strength (the method has not memorised the manuscript) and a weakness (a fine-tuned model might extract finer-grained distinctions than we report).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The foundation model\u8217's training-corpus class bounds what the lens can recognise.} As flagged in \u167 ?3.2, the foundation model is a contemporary large-scale vision-language model trained on web-scraped image\u8211-text pairs, and its training corpus is Western / internet-era / English-language weighted. In particular, non-Western pre-modern visual conventions may be under-represented in the lens\u8217's \u8220"sight\u8221": a fifteenth-century central-European illustrator drawing on visual idioms the contemporary internet captions poorly (e.g.\u160 ?specific alchemical or humoral conventions, Germanic herbal traditions under-represented in English-language botanical image corpora) is a case where the lens\u8217's recognition capacity is narrower than its dimension labels suggest. The dimensions that discriminate most strongly in \u167 ?5.2 \u8212- {\i herbal/botanical}, {\i celestial/astronomical}, {\i aquatic/fluid} \u8212- are precisely the ones whose training-corpus coverage is densest; the dimensions that discriminate less sharply ({\i alchemical transformation}, {\i ritualistic/ceremonial}) are dimensions whose training-corpus coverage is likely thinner and less canonical. We cannot cleanly separate a {\i method-limit} reading (the lens sees alchemical mood weakly) from a {\i content-limit} reading (the manuscript carries alchemical mood diffusely, \u167 ?6.3). We flag the confound rather than resolve it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Section labels are scholarly consensus, not ground truth.} The five-section partition (and the six-section refinement reported in \u167 ?5.7) is stable but not inviolable. We use it as a benchmark because it is the best-available external reference; we do not treat it as correct by construction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\i n}\u8196 ?=\u8196 ?12 for astronomical.} The LOOCV classification of astronomical pages is 12 / 12, point estimate 100%, Wilson 95% confidence interval [75.8%, 100%]. The point estimate is perfect but the confidence interval is wide because the sample size is small. A twelfth page is a twelfth page, not a thousand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b We do not disclose the foundation model.} Replication by a third party with a different vision-language model will produce different exact numbers. We expect the {\i qualitative} result \u8212- every dimension discriminates, the classifier clears 85%, pharmaceutical confuses with herbal, all three lenses clear 80%, the raw embedding slightly out-performs the 16-d projection \u8212- to be robust across any sufficiently capable contemporary VLM.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Out-of-distribution manuscript control is preliminary.} \u167 ?5.9 reports a small-sample OOD probe against the Tacuinum Sanitatis (n = 3 pages, limited by a Wikimedia Commons rate-limit), the Codex Seraphinianus (n = 30), and the Rohonc Codex (n = 30). The qualitative pattern is exactly what a legitimate medieval-codex content lens should produce \u8212- the Tacuinum peaks on herbal/pharmaceutical/fertility, the two undeciphered-script codices peak on encoded/hidden \u8212- but the Tacuinum sample is too small for a statistical claim. A larger Tacuinum Sanitatis + Carrara Herbal + Naples Dioscorides baseline is an obvious forthcoming task; we expect the qualitative pattern to strengthen with larger {\i n}, not reverse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b No human-expert inter-rater evaluation.} We lean on the rhetorical move that the radar profiles in Figure 2 are {\i right for the right reason} \u8212- legible to a trained Voynich reader as matching what they would predict. This is a rhetorical argument from interpretability, not a measurement. A full study would show the profiles blind to a panel of Voynich scholars and measure inter-rater agreement against the system\u8217's output. We do not do that here. The argument from legibility rests on the reader\u8217's judgement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Text-channel baseline is present but uses a small corpus.} \u167 ?5.10 reports a Voynichese character-n-gram classifier trained on the 182-page intersection of the visual corpus and Takahashi\u8217's complete transcription. The result \u8212- 92.3% vs the 16-d visual channel\u8217's 92.3% \u8212- is essentially a tie. This is a new finding in the paper and is worth stating again: the visual and text channels recover the section structure at equivalent accuracy on this corpus, and the 16-d archetype projection neither outperforms nor is outperformed by Currier-style character statistics. The visual channel\u8217's advantage is not accuracy; it is that the resulting profile can be read in English.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b {\f1 encoded_hidden} is partially a layout detector.} The recipes section scores highest on {\i encoded/hidden} because its pages are dominated by dense Voynichese script. The dimension descriptor was written to capture \u8220"mysterious script arranged in lines and paragraphs,\u8221" and a text-dense page of {\i any} script (Latin, Cyrillic, a modern invented alphabet) would score similarly high. The dimension is therefore partially a detector of \u8220"dense unfamiliar script on parchment\u8221" rather than a pure semantic meaning channel. We flag this explicitly rather than leaving it to the reader to infer. The rest of the sixteen dimensions do not have this issue.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Preprocessing and photographic-style robustness have not been ablated.} The Beinecke IIIF pages vary in dimensions and photographic lighting. We do not report whether the profile distances correlate with folio-number adjacency (which would indicate a photographic-batch confound). We expect the correlation to be weak \u8212- the sections are much more geometrically distinctive than any photographic batch \u8212- but we have not measured it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The load-bearing dimensionality-reduction panel is PCA, not UMAP.} \u167 ?5.5 reports both UMAP and a deterministic linear PCA projection. UMAP is a visualisation aid that depends on hyperparameters ({\f1 n_neighbors}, {\f1 min_dist}, random seed) and whose exact cluster geometry is not unique; we report a 10-seed Procrustes stability check in Figure 9 but stop short of a full hyperparameter grid. The claim that the sections separate in 16-d archetype space is carried by the PCA panel, the ANOVA + classifier results, and the cross-section distance matrix \u8212- all of which are deterministic. A reader who is sceptical of UMAP should read the PCA panel as the primary geometric evidence and UMAP as illustrative.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.7 What a 16-dimensional representation forecloses\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Drucker\u8217's critique of datafication (Drucker 2014) asks what is lost when a humanistic object is represented as a numerical vector. The question applies directly here. A 16-d archetype profile says that a page of the Voynich Manuscript lands at a particular position in a human-authored sixteen-dimensional thematic space; it does not say anything about the brushstroke, the pigment, the palaeographic hand, the quire position (Fagin Davis\u8217's 2020 codicological analysis of gathering structure remains the necessary manuscript-studies substrate for any richer reading), the marginalia, the codicological seam between gatherings, or the thousand other properties of the physical object a human reader would notice. Each is a distinct {\i foreclosure event} \u8212- the representation actively refuses to say anything about it, not because such things are unimportant but because the sixteen-axis vocabulary was authored for a different purpose. The representation is useful because it compresses page-level content into a form that quantitative analysis can operate on; the representation is impoverished because it cannot say anything that is not already in one of its sixteen axes. We foreground this limit rather than bury it. What the quantitative analysis in \u167 ?5 demonstrates is that the sixteen-axis representation is {\i sufficient} to recover the manuscript\u8217's scholarly section structure. It does not demonstrate that the sixteen-axis representation is {\i adequate} to the manuscript in any richer sense. The richer reading \u8212- the codicology, the palaeography, the iconographic detail \u8212- remains the domain of art-historical and manuscript-studies scholarship (\u167 ?2.3), which our method complements but does not replace.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We also acknowledge a second-order foreclosure: the foundation model that grounds the perception layer is a political-economic artefact as well as a technical one. The training corpus reflects the labour and licensing decisions of the entities that assembled it; the choice of a proprietary closed-weight model over an open-weight alternative (see \u167 ?3.2 on the disclosed model class) reflects our own commercial commitments as much as a technical judgement. Critical AI scholarship (Bender et al. 2021; Birhane et al. 2022) has argued that treating a foundation model as a neutral instrument misreads it. We agree; the sixteen-dimensional profile is not a view {\i of} the manuscript from nowhere but a view {\i through} a specific instrument whose provenance we have partially withheld. Readers should read the empirical results in \u167 ?5 alongside that limit rather than despite it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.8 Implications for undeciphered and context-stripped documents\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If the Voynich\u8217's visual channel is this rich, other undeciphered or under-analysed illustrated manuscripts are plausibly tractable to the same treatment. The Codex Seraphinianus (modern, intentionally opaque), the Rohonc Codex (eighteenth-century Hungarian, undeciphered), and illuminated manuscripts whose text is damaged or lost are candidate targets. More broadly, the same analysis applies to any visual document where the text is inaccessible \u8212- whether because the script is undeciphered, the language is dead, or the context has been stripped from archaeological or archival fragments. We bound this claim explicitly to European medieval codices and their near-contemporaries: the training-corpus provenance of the foundation model (\u167 ?3.2, \u167 ?6.6) means the archetype lens is best-calibrated for objects the modern web already indexes densely; non-Western pre-modern visual traditions (Timbuktu manuscripts, palm-leaf codices, Mesoamerican pictorial documents) would require corpus-specific lens redesign and calibration before comparable claims could be made. The Voynich is an extreme case within the European medieval-codex tradition. The principle is more general than the calibration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 6.9 What a practitioner does with this\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paper is a methodological demonstration; it is only useful to the humanities community if the released dataset lets scholars, teachers, and curators do things they could not do before. We list four concrete uses drawn from the constituencies who have historically cared about the Voynich Manuscript.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Manuscript-studies research.} The per-page 16-d profile vectors, distributed at Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.19560769 under CC BY-SA 4.0, allow a researcher to run cross-corpus comparisons against any other digitised medieval codex whose imagery has been indexed by the same or an equivalent visual encoder. A researcher asking whether the Voynich\u8217's herbal illustrations sit closer to the Tacuinum Sanitatis tradition, the Carrara Herbal tradition, or an idiosyncratic contemporary hand can operationalise that question against the released vectors without re-running the profile-generation pipeline. The \u167 ?5.9 out-of-distribution probe demonstrates the pattern; the released dataset lets others push it further.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Digital-humanities pedagogy.} The dataset is small enough to fit in a graduate seminar\u8217's shared drive and rich enough to support a semester\u8217's methodological instruction. Its structure \u8212- sixteen human-authored dimensions \u215 ? 197 pages \u215 ? a public foundation-model substrate \u8212- makes it an unusually clean object on which to teach classification, permutation testing, ablation logic, lens-specificity control, and honest disclosure of foundation-model limits. We have designed the release with pedagogical clarity in mind; every figure in the paper is regeneratable from the public artefact using the published analysis script (Appendix B).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Curatorial practice.} Curators of Beinecke MS 408 and its scholarly context have historically had no quantitative handle on the manuscript\u8217's imagery. The released profiles are one available handle. A curator preparing an exhibit or a scholarly catalogue can use the per-page vectors to organise the imagery by its computationally-measured thematic register, or to identify pages whose profile diverges from their scholarly section for exhibit discussion. We make no claim that the quantitative register replaces or should replace the curatorial judgement; we claim only that it is now available as one input among many.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Cross-community research infrastructure.} The paper\u8217's method is not proprietary to xenoglyph; only the specific profile-generation pipeline is (\u167 ?3.2). Any researcher with a comparable vision-language model and the willingness to author their own dimension descriptors can run an equivalent analysis on their own object of interest. We view the released dataset less as a definitive account of the Voynich Manuscript than as a template that the digital-humanities community can adapt to its own corpora.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The paper does not argue that computational visual-semantic profiling should displace or replace any existing humanistic mode of engaging with the manuscript. The point is complementarity, not substitution. Quantitative measurement of what a picture is {\i about} is one tool among many that a scholar, teacher, or curator of illustrated manuscripts might reach for. We hope this paper\u8217's contribution is that this particular tool, applied to this particular manuscript, produced a result that is worth having on the record.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 7. Conclusion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have presented the first systematic {\i computational} visual semantic analysis of the Voynich Manuscript, and the first non-textual independent validation of its scholarly section taxonomy. All sixteen of a general-purpose medieval-codex archetype lens discriminate between the manuscript\u8217's five scholarly sections at {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?15}. A Pipeline-wrapped multinomial logistic classifier on the resulting 16-d profiles recovers scholarly section assignments with 90.4% leave-one-out accuracy (Wilson 95% CI [85.4%, 93.7%]; permutation {\i p}\u8196 ?<\u8196 ?10{\super \u8722 ?3}), failing measurably only on the herbal-pharmaceutical boundary where the manuscript itself is ambiguous. The raw 768-d foundation-model embedding slightly out-performs the 16-d projection; a six-variable low-level layout-feature baseline recovers the sections at 72%; two unrelated 16-d archetype lenses also recover the sections at \u8805 ? 84%. The illustrations of the Voynich Manuscript are not decorative, they are not uniform, and they are not unreadable by a modern image-understanding model \u8212- they are thematically organised in a way that is machine-measurable, interpretation-ready, independent of the lens one chooses to view them through, and consistent with six hundred years of scholarly intuition about the manuscript\u8217's structure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 We have not deciphered the Voynich Manuscript. We have, we believe, put the first numbers on a narrower question: {\i what dimensions of the manuscript\u8217's illustration programme are computationally accessible from pixels alone?} The answer, on the sixteen-dimensional archetype vocabulary we authored for this analysis, is: the scholarly section taxonomy is recoverable at 90.4% leave-one-out accuracy from the visual channel alone. This is a claim about section structure being computationally accessible, not a claim about \u8220"meaning\u8221" in any broader humanistic sense. Section structure survives; whether the manuscript\u8217's {\i meaning} \u8212- its authorial intent, its intended readership, its relation to the medieval or early-modern intellectual traditions from which its imagery draws \u8212- survives is a question our method is not equipped to answer. That question remains, as it has for six centuries, the domain of scholarly interpretation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Future work falls into three natural directions. First, we intend to apply the complementary {\i dimension discovery} mode to the same corpus, in which the system proposes latent semantic dimensions from the pixels alone without human-authored descriptors \u8212- a zero-prior version of the present analysis that can be compared against the supervised-lens result. Second, we intend to extend the analysis to other under-analysed illustrated European medieval codices (the Rohonc Codex, damaged early-medieval herbals) to measure whether the discrimination pattern we observe on the Voynich generalises within the tradition for which the archetype lens is calibrated. Third, and provisionally, we hope the released dataset (Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.19560769) will support the practitioner uses discussed in \u167 ?6.9 \u8212- cross-corpus manuscript-studies research, digital-humanities pedagogy, and curatorial reflection \u8212- regardless of whether our own future work continues.\par}
